I think that you may be overstating the case.  The fact that some
commodities now used for trade will decrease radically in real value does
not rule out all trade.  It should carry on in currencies that retain or
increase their value.  Nor does it preclude the possibility that trade in
old currencies will continue, albeit under the influence of vast inflation.
 Even with strong nanotech, I may prefer to have get a new aircar *now* in
exchange for my artwork, rather than waiting for my own nanotech assemblers
to grow one.

>How can fakes be determined?  Won't we be able to reproduce anything we
>can perceive? . . .
>Christopher L. Weeks

Not necessarily.  By definition, we won't be able to reproduce *real*
artifacts.  We can only make copies.  Yes, there will be increased costs to
authenticate artifacts, but as I argued earlier this will only serve to
reallocate values between different types of artifacts.  Those that are
sufficently authenticated will increase in value relative to those are
quite suspect.  There are a number a methods by which authentication could
advance, one of which you have outlined (registry).  Others will no doubt
arise as market pressures for valid authentication increase.  

By the way, I've thought up another commodity that should retain its value:
 personal services.  To the extent that VR and AI can copy meat
consciousness, the value of this commodity will also raise authentication
problems.  But I imagine there will always be connoiseurs who value
personal realspace services.  Look for the purported "world's oldest
business" to continue to thrive.

The lesson:  invest now in a sealed time capsule!  What you unseal in 2100
before the eyes of artifact collectors will buy you entire planets, or the
equivalent value in the personal services of a live hyperband, meaty
transprostitute, or gen-u-ine 20th century Coke can.


