From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: DEF: Revolt vs Revolution

Although I've only been skimming the "revolution" posts, it appears to me
that they are all talking about "revolt"--not "revolution".

As the word plainly implies, a revolution is in some sense a return to
previous conditions.  The term originally was applied during the 1600 in
Britain, as political combattants sought to justify their positions with an
appeal to time-honored values.  Much the same was true of how the U.S.
revolutionaries viewed their struggle; they saw it as an attempt to reclaim
the British liberties that were their birthright.

Libertarians today can thus claim to be revolutionaries, as they seek in
large part to reinstate the Founders' political values.

Jesus of Nazareth might thus also qualify as a revolutionary, as he
sometimes couched his theology in Old Testament terms.

But the rioters in LA were not revolutionaries.  Nor were the inventors of
the atomic bomb.  These folks just effected changes--not revolutions.


