Date: Wed, 3 Feb 93 11:36:57 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Zero-sum games

Hal Finney writes:
>Having gone through this analysis, though, I am convinced that the
>game theory definition of zero-sum does not capture the differences
>people have in mind when they compare the futures and stock markets,
>saying "The futures market is zero-sum, but the stock market is better
>because it's positive-sum."  (I think it was some claim like this which
>started the whole debate.)

What is "zero-sum" depends subtly one what exactly one means by this
phrase; which is why I tried (and failed) to get proponents of this
claim to offer a precise definition.

>		A		B
>	A	(110,90)	(96,104)
>	B	(100,100)	(99,101)
>
>		A		B
>	A	(10,-10)	(-4,4)
>	B	(0,0)		(-1,1)
>
>... Looking at these three games, there is a real
>sense in which the first is positive-sum, the second zero-sum ...
>Looking back at the futures market versus stock market, then, the first
>two game matrices above described typical transactions in such
>markets.

Well you have also not offered a precise definition, but it seems you
are trying to distinguish the average return in the two markets.
However, if we take these numbers to be dollars, these two market
games could not co-exist as options in a larger market without
arbitrage making competitive their (risk-adjusted) average returns.
Consider your example: "One year from now, I will deliver one ounce of
gold to Robin in exchange for $450." If we both believed the price
would be $450 then, the price of such a contract now would *not* be
$450.  Rather, the price would be less due to our expectations about
being able to make a return on our money in the meantime, return which
we might get in the stock market if we wanted.

Read any standard textbook on finance, and you will see that returns
from commodity futures should be competitive, and in fact historically
have been competitive, when adjusted for risk.  A neutral portfolio
can profit from having both futures and equities together.  I really
am not making a new claim; I am trying to communicate established
wisdom in finance.  

Robin Hanson

