Date: Wed, 3 Feb 1993 18:03:31 +0000
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: ECON/NANO: A short question

Ronald Hale-Evans writes:
>What, if any, objects, substances, or other entities, will still be in
>short supply and therefore valuable as commodities, in a world with
>"strong" nanotech (i.e. assemblers, etc.)?

Time.  Even with accelerated uploads, we will still, relatively, have the
same amount of time as each other on the whole, although with a lot more
variation.

Tom Morrow writes:
>Good new ideas.  Strong nanotech will no doubt help us to generate,
>transmit, and store information more efficiently, but it will have only
>indirect effects on creativity.  Look for art and inventions (whether

A good idea may be valuable, but once it has been communicated, it can be
duplicated free, and therefore is not a valuable commodity.  I'm assuming
that after strong nanotech, if not before, attempts to protect intellectual
property will be futile.  There may be some room for argument over this,
but I don't want to argue it right now.

On the other hand, the ability to have lots of good ideas is valuable, but
it is not something which can be passed around like a physical good.  All
that can be used as a medium of commerce is claims on the
artist/inventor/scientist's time.  Which brings us back to time.

Christopher L. Weeks writes:
>Now that we have established what the valuable commodities are, how will
>I purchase them from you?

By convincing me that you are a person with whom it will be a pleasure to
associate.  When the necessities and all the present-day luxuries of life
are virtually free, the truly valuable must be puchased other than with
money.

In certain spheres of life this happens already.  If you write a
mathematical paper, and want to have another mathematician read it and
comment, the surest way to get this to happen is to have a reputation that
will persuade the other mathematician that it will be worth while for him. 
The more eminent he is, the more eminent you must be to get his attention. 
Eminent authors reply to fan mail with form letters, if at all.  Many of
the greatest spiritual teachers (I here leave aside the question of their
authenticity) would place great obstacles in the way of any would-be pupil,
to weed out those who would be merely wasting the guru's time.

I predict that personal relationships will be the currency of a
post-nanotech society.

