> What, if any, objects, substances, or other entities, will still be in
> short supply and therefore valuable as commodities, in a world with
> "strong" nanotech (i.e. assemblers, etc.)?

Energy and raw materials would likely remain in short supply, and anything
made from them. Admittedly, humans would be able to build dyson spheres,
but I suspect that usage of energy per person will expand so long as
there is more energy available. Raw materials would continue to be
limited in supply -- there is a limit to how many people can turn Jupiter
into a computer to upload into, as there aren't that many Jupiters to
go around. Of course, predicting precisely what may end up being in
short supply and what won't gets hard, but in general, look to anything
thats got a conservation law associated with it in physics and it will
be rare again someday -- that means matter and energy.

